<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">Android and iOS need the same checkout rules, while each team wants to keep its own screen. That is already a valid sharing boundary. A later decision to share the screen adds host, navigation, input and accessibility responsibilities.</p>

Compare those boundaries by following one feature through its owners, rather than treating all shared code as one architectural choice.

<!-- @after Paradigm B: Shared UI (Compose Multiplatform) :: visual -->
### Move the boundary deliberately

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Logic-only sharing</strong><small>Shared rules and data, separately owned native UI</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Selected shared screens</strong><small>One shared flow inside explicit native hosts</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Platform capabilities</strong><small>SDK calls, permissions and resources retain target owners</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Release acceptance</strong><small>Both apps must meet their product requirements</small></div></div><figcaption>Moving a composable into commonMain does not decide who owns a native camera or a screen back stack.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after The Boundary Pattern: Inversion of Control :: animation -->
### A location request crosses a small seam

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">A location request crosses a small seam</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Shared feature</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>Location contract</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Platform adapter</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Owner</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Need", "caption": "The feature needs a location outcome to fetch weather. The domain does not need an Android Context or an Apple controller.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Needs coordinates", "v1": "Outcome contract", "v2": "Not selected yet", "v3": "Screen/flow lifetime"}}, {"label": "Construct", "caption": "The platform composition root supplies a matching adapter. Its permission and accuracy policy must be explicit.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Receives dependency", "v1": "Common types", "v2": "Platform SDK integration", "v3": "Construction belongs here"}}, {"label": "Run", "caption": "The caller starts the operation under an explicit owner. The adapter translates callbacks and failures into the agreed outcome.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Awaits outcome", "v1": "Documented semantics", "v2": "Owns resources", "v3": "Owns cancellation"}}, {"label": "Dispose", "caption": "When that owner ends, the integration must release its observation/resources according to the platform contract. Test both apps.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "No stale delivery promised", "v1": "Contract tested", "v2": "Cleanup must be verified", "v3": "Lifecycle test"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Must sharing rules also share the UI?</summary><p>No. Logic-only sharing and shared UI are different product boundaries.</p></details>
<details><summary>Who owns a shared ViewModel on iOS?</summary><p>The integration must supply and retain the intended owner and clear its store at that lifetime boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why avoid platform-shaped interfaces?</summary><p>They can force the other target to promise semantics its OS does not provide.</p></details>


